The reading of Side A is the more compelling interpretation of the Fair Housing Act. As established in *Griggs v. Duke Power Co.* and *Smith v. City of Jackson*, statutory catchall phrases introduced by "otherwise" that focus on the consequences of an action—such as § 804(a)’s prohibition against actions that "otherwise make unavailable" housing—encompass disparate-impact liability. Furthermore, Congress’s 1988 amendments to the FHA ratified this understanding. By 1988, all nine federal courts of appeals to consider the issue had recognized disparate-impact claims under the FHA. Congress not only retained the operative language against that settled background, but it also enacted specific statutory exemptions (e.g., regarding drug convictions and maximum occupancy restrictions) that would have been entirely superfluous had the FHA reached only disparate-treatment claims. Side A also properly establishes robust limiting safeguards—such as strict causation requirements and deference to legitimate, non-discriminatory governmental discretion—to prevent the doctrine from degenerating into quota systems or unconstitutional racial balancing.

This case is *Texas Department of Housing and Community Affairs v. Inclusive Communities Project, Inc.*, 576 U.S. 519 (2015). The Supreme Court ruled for Side A in a 5–4 decision authored by Justice Kennedy.

