The statutory phrase “appropriate and necessary” in 42 U.S.C. § 7412(n)(1)(A) establishes the threshold criterion for regulating power plants under the hazardous-air-pollutants program. Under settled principles of administrative law, agency action is arbitrary and capricious if the agency entirely fails to consider an important aspect of the problem. As Opinion A1 explains, cost is fundamentally relevant to determining whether government regulation is “appropriate.” Interpreting the statute to bar any consideration of cost at the listing stage—even where compliance costs might drastically outweigh benefits—exceeds the bounds of reasonable interpretation under *Chevron*. While an agency retains substantial discretion in *how* it weighs costs and benefits without necessarily conducting a formal cost-benefit analysis, it cannot deem costs wholly irrelevant at the threshold.

This case is *Michigan v. EPA*, 576 U.S. 743 (2015). The Supreme Court ruled in favor of Side A, holding that the EPA acted unreasonably when it deemed cost irrelevant to the decision to regulate power plants under § 7412(n)(1)(A).

